 ],
 "literal": "I turned [her] to embrace, 'I sweated,' she said. / Now I knew that to be [her] aversion. / In the cloud-crawling Potiyil of Ay of the hero's anklet and armlet, / smelling of vēṅkai and kāntal, / she herself is cooler than even the water-lily flower.",
 "translation": "When I turned her to embrace her, she said, 'I have sweated' [and drew back]. Only now do I understand that it was her aversion to my touch. She, who is fragrant with the vēṅkai and kāntal flowers of the cloud-crawling Potiyil mountain of the chieftain Āy who wears hero's anklets and armlets, is herself cooler and gentler than even the water-lily flower.",
 "modern": "நான் அவளைத் தழுவுவதற்காகத் திருப்பியபோது, 'எனக்கு வேர்த்துவிட்டது' என்று அவள் கூறினாள். அது என் மீதான அவளது வெறுப்பு (விலகல்) என்பதை இப்போதுதான் நான் உணர்ந்துகொண்டேன். வீரக்கழலும் தோள்வளையும் அணிந்த ஆய் மன்னனின், மேகங்கள் தவழும் பொதிய மலையில் மலரும் வேங்கை மற்றும் காந்தள் மலர்களின் நறுமணத்தைக் கொண்டவளும், ஆம்பல் மலரை விடவும் குளுமையானவளுமாகிய என் மகள், இப்போது என்னிடமிருந்து விலகிவிட்டாள்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the foster-mother (cevili) after the heroine has eloped with her lover. She recalls a past incident when she tried to embrace her daughter, but the daughter shrank back, claiming she was sweating. The foster-mother now realizes that this was not mere physical sweating, but an expression of her daughter's growing aversion (tuṉi) to her touch, as her heart was already entirely devoted to her lover. The daughter is described as being fragrant with the vēṅkai and kāntal flowers of the Potiyil mountain of the chieftain Āy, and as being cooler and gentler than a water-lily. This contrast between her natural coolness and her sudden 'sweating' highlights the emotional turmoil and secrecy of her secret love before her elopement.",
